{
  "result": "The first thing Mara bought at the estate sale was a kitchen timer shaped like a tomato. She had come for a desk, but the desk was already sold, and the timer had a strip of masking tape on its underside. In blue ink someone had written: FOR DOING NOTHING.\n\nAt home she set it for ten minutes. Almost immediately she noticed a crumb on the table. She moved to brush it away, then wondered whether that counted. The crumb had been there since breakfast. It seemed capable of waiting. She put both hands flat on her knees and listened.\n\nThe refrigerator clicked off. Upstairs, a child practiced the same four notes on a flute, with a long pause before the fourth. A delivery truck stopped outside, and somebody called, \u201cNot that door,\u201d with surprising tenderness. Then the truck went away, and the child got the fourth note exactly right.\n\nWhen the timer rang, Mara felt the small disappointment she used to feel when a teacher switched on the lights after a film. She brushed the crumb into her palm. It was a bit of toast crust, not quite large enough to account for how much attention it had required.\n\nThe next afternoon she set the timer again. This time the flute never started. Rain ran down the window in two thin lines that met halfway down. She watched the joined line gather speed. When the bell rang, she turned the tomato over and added a second strip: NOT FINISHED.",
